---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import IdealWeightCalculator from '@components/islands/IdealWeightCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'ideal-weight-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Which ideal weight formula is best?',
    answer:
      'There is no single "correct" formula. The Robinson, Miller, Devine and Hamwi equations were each developed for different purposes and give slightly different results. Treat the spread of values as a healthy range rather than one exact target.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is ideal body weight calculated?',
    answer:
      'Each formula starts from a base weight at 5 feet of height and adds a fixed amount per inch above that. The Devine formula, for example, is 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft for men (45.5 kg for women), so a 5\'10" man works out to about 73 kg (161 lb).',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use this for a child?',
    answer:
      'The calculator accepts ages 2 to 80, but the four formulas were all derived on adults, so below 18 it tells you they do not apply rather than printing a number. For children and teenagers, healthy weight is assessed from BMI-for-age percentiles — a child\'s body fat changes with age, and boys and girls differ as they mature — so use the BMI calculator instead.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do the formulas only use height and sex?',
    answer:
      'These classic formulas estimate a reference weight from height and sex alone. They do not account for body frame, muscle mass or age, which is why the healthy-BMI range is also shown for context.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much should I weigh for my height?',
    answer:
      'Rather than one number, aim for the range that corresponds to a healthy BMI of 18.5 to 25 for your height, which the calculator shows as its own row alongside the formula estimates. A wider frame or more muscle can place a healthy weight toward or above the top of that range.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is ideal weight the same as a healthy weight?',
    answer:
      'Not exactly. "Ideal body weight" formulas were originally created for medication dosing and clinical use. A healthy weight is better described as a range — which is why we also show the weights corresponding to a BMI of 18.5 to 25.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  rail
  intro="Estimate your ideal body weight from age, gender and height using four established formulas (Robinson, Miller, Devine, Hamwi), plus the healthy weight range for your height."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-28' }}
>
  <IdealWeightCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>US Units</strong> or <strong>Metric Units</strong>. Switching converts a height you have already typed rather than clearing it.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>age</strong> and select your <strong>gender</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>height</strong> — feet and inches in US units, centimetres in metric.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see the four ideal-weight estimates side by side, plus the healthy weight range for your height.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Other Units</strong> opens a small converter above the calculator. It is not a third
      unit system: use it when your scales read in stones or your tape reads in metres, then type
      the converted figure into the boxes. The calculator stays on whichever system you chose.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it asks your age</h2>
    <p>
      Age changes none of the four formulas — they use height and sex only. It decides whether they
      apply at all. All four were derived on adults, so below 18 they are the wrong instrument, and
      the calculator says so instead of printing a number. For children and teenagers, healthy
      weight is judged from BMI-for-age percentiles, which account for the way body composition
      changes as a child grows; the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a> is the tool
      for that.
    </p>

    <h2>What "ideal body weight" means</h2>
    <p>
      Ideal body weight (IBW) formulas estimate a reference weight from height and sex alone. They
      were originally devised for clinical purposes such as medication dosing, not as a personal
      goal. That history is worth remembering: the output is a useful reference point, not a verdict
      on what you should weigh.
    </p>

    <h2>The four formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Each formula sets a base weight for a 5-foot (60-inch) frame and adds a fixed amount for every
      inch above that. They differ only in those two constants. For men, the figures are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Robinson (1983):</strong> 52 kg + 1.9 kg per inch over 5 ft</li>
      <li><strong>Miller (1983):</strong> 56.2 kg + 1.41 kg per inch over 5 ft</li>
      <li><strong>Devine (1974):</strong> 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft</li>
      <li><strong>Hamwi (1964):</strong> 48 kg + 2.7 kg per inch over 5 ft</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The women's versions use slightly lower base weights and increments. Below 5 ft, each formula falls back to its base weight.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>For a man who is <strong>5 ft 10 in</strong> (10 inches over 5 ft), the four formulas give:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Formula</th><th>Estimate</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Miller</td><td>70.3 kg (155 lb)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Robinson</td><td>71.0 kg (157 lb)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Devine</td><td>73.0 kg (161 lb)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hamwi</td><td>75.0 kg (165 lb)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Devine, for instance, is 50 + 2.3 × 10 = 73 kg. The four land within about five kilograms of
      each other — close enough to read as a band rather than four competing answers.
    </p>

    <h2>The healthy-BMI range for context</h2>
    <p>
      Alongside the formulas, the calculator shows the weights that correspond to a BMI of 18.5 to
      25 at your height — the bottom and the top of the healthy band. For 5 ft 10 in that is
      <strong>128.9 lb to 174.2 lb</strong> (about 58.5–79.0 kg): a wider span than the formulas
      give, and a reminder that healthy weight is a range, not a single point.
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond height and sex</h2>
    <p>
      These formulas ignore frame size, muscle mass and age, so they fit some bodies better than
      others. A broad-framed or muscular person can be healthy above the estimates, while a slight
      frame may sit below them. Use the numbers as a guide, cross-check against the BMI range, and
      treat neither as a rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      To screen your current weight against height, use the
      <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a>; to gauge composition rather than weight,
      see the <a href="/health/body-fat-calculator">body fat calculator</a>. This is educational
      information, not medical advice — talk to a healthcare provider about a target that is right
      for you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
